<ol><li><strong>Chapter 1 · supplied PDF pp. 743–748</strong><small>The devas’ fear of Taraka, their arrival at Kailasa and Shiva’s answer.</small></li><li><strong>Chapter 2 · supplied PDF pp. 748–754</strong><small>Agni carries the radiance; the six Krittikas, Himavat and Ganga pass it onward; the child appears among the reeds.</small></li><li><strong>Chapter 3 · supplied PDF pp. 755–758</strong><small>Vishvamitra performs the child’s rites; Agni gives the spear; the Krittikas foster Kartikeya and he reveals six faces.</small></li><li><strong>Chapter 4 · supplied PDF pp. 759–764</strong><small>Shiva and Parvati seek the child; Nandin arrives; Kartikeya honours the Krittikas and accepts the invitation to Kailasa.</small></li><li><strong>Chapter 5 · supplied PDF pp. 765–769</strong><small>Kartikeya’s homecoming, reunion with Shiva and Parvati, consecration and gifts from the divine assembly.</small></li></ol>
<p>The text’s intimate and reproductive language is condensed as “divine radiance” in the main reader while the source sequence remains explicit here. The later war with Taraka is left for another story. Regional Murugan traditions and alternative Puranic birth accounts are acknowledged without being merged. Three commissioned artworks are AI-generated devotional interpretations; the settings and figures are imagined.</p>
